{
  "id": "accusative-pronouns",
  "level": "A1",
  "title": "Akkusativpronomen",
  "group": "Cases",
  "sourceFile": "akkuspronom.html",
  "overviewSource": "akkuspronomenexpl.html",
  "overview": {
    "blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": "Personal pronouns change form when they are the direct object of a verb. At A1 the most noticeable changes are ich → mich, du → dich, er → ihn, and wir → uns / ihr → euch."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "content": "When to use accusative"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": "The accusative marks the direct object — whoever or whatever receives the action. Ask: \"Who or what is being verbed?\" If the pronoun does the action, it stays nominative (Er kommt). If it receives the action, use accusative (Ich sehe ihn)."
      },
      {
        "type": "example",
        "de": "Kennst du meine Schwester? — Nein, ich kenne sie nicht.",
        "note": "sie (her) = direct object of kennen"
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "content": "Verbs that take an accusative object"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": "Many transitive verbs take a person or thing in the accusative. These appear often in this chapter:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "content": "haben (have)\nkennen (know someone)\nlieben (love)\nsehen (see)\nbesuchen (visit)\nanrufen (call)\nfinden (find; think, find sth. + adj.)\nkaufen (buy)\nnehmen (take)\ngeben (give)\nwaschen (wash)\nrufen (call out to)"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": "Verbs like sein, heißen, and wohnen do not take an accusative person as a direct object. Location uses a preposition: Sie wohnt in Berlin."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": "Accusative pronouns also follow accusative prepositions: für mich, ohne dich, durch uns…"
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "content": "Pronoun forms"
      },
      {
        "type": "table",
        "content": "",
        "rows": [
          [
            "",
            "Nom.",
            "Acc."
          ],
          [
            "ich",
            "ich",
            "mich"
          ],
          [
            "du",
            "du",
            "dich"
          ],
          [
            "er/sie/es",
            "er/sie/es",
            "ihn/sie/es"
          ],
          [
            "wir",
            "wir",
            "uns"
          ],
          [
            "ihr",
            "ihr",
            "euch"
          ],
          [
            "sie/Sie",
            "sie/Sie",
            "sie/Sie"
          ]
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "example",
        "de": "Siehst du mich?",
        "note": "mich = accusative of ich"
      },
      {
        "type": "example",
        "de": "Ich liebe dich.",
        "note": "dich = accusative of du"
      },
      {
        "type": "example",
        "de": "Meine Tante wohnt in Milwaukee. Ich besuche sie morgen.",
        "note": "sie (her) after besuchen"
      }
    ]
  },
